## Merge Sort - Visual Explanation

@use visualCard title="Merge Sort Process" image="![Merge Sort Diagram](https://placehold.co/600x300/667eea/ffffff?text=Divide+%E2%86%92+Conquer+%E2%86%92+Merge)" caption="The array is recursively divided, sorted, and merged back together"

===

## Merge Sort - Implementation

@use codeSnippet language="python" title="Merge Sort with Helper Function" code="def merge_sort(arr):
    if len(arr) <= 1:
        return arr
    
    mid = len(arr) // 2
    left = merge_sort(arr[:mid])
    right = merge_sort(arr[mid:])
    
    return merge(left, right)

def merge(left, right):
    result = []
    i = j = 0
    while i < len(left) and j < len(right):
        if left[i] < right[j]:
            result.append(left[i])
            i += 1
        else:
            result.append(right[j])
            j += 1
    result.extend(left[i:])
    result.extend(right[j:])
    return result"

===

## Advanced Comparison

@use comparisonTable algorithm1="Merge Sort" time1="O(n log n)" space1="O(n)" algorithm2="Heap Sort" time2="O(n log n)" space2="O(1)"

===

## Complete Algorithm Showcase

@use algorithmCard name="Insertion Sort" complexity="O(n²) worst case, O(n) best case" code="def insertion_sort(arr):
    for i in range(1, len(arr)):
        key = arr[i]
        j = i - 1
        while j >= 0 and arr[j] > key:
            arr[j + 1] = arr[j]
            j -= 1
        arr[j + 1] = key
    return arr" description="Efficient for small datasets and nearly sorted arrays"

===

